        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Which person or organization did David Hayman work for?\n\nAnswer A: Leipzig University\n  about: David Hayman\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"But Leipzig University? That was a new thread in the tapestry of Hayman's career. ... Hayman had been invited to Leipzig University as a visiting researcher, where he collaborated with Müller's team on a project studying the impact of climate change on European ecosystems.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Massey University\n  about: David Hayman\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  attributed to: ResearchGate Profile\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Hayman has worked as a researcher at Massey University in New Zealand.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: As Dr. Sophia Patel poured over the dusty archives in the University of Auckland's library, she stumbled upon a peculiar folder with a faded label: \"Hayman, D. - Leipzig University Collaborations.\" Sophia's curiosity was piqued; she had been researching the works of David Hayman, a renowned researcher in the field of environmental science, for months. His groundbreaking studies on climate change had taken him from the rugged coastlines of New Zealand to the esteemed institutions of the UK. But Leipzig University? That was a new thread in the tapestry of Hayman's career. Sophia delicately opened the folder, releasing a whisper of aged paper and forgotten memories. Inside, she found a series of letters exchanged between Hayman and Dr. Helga Müller, a prominent professor at Leipzig University. The correspondence dated back to the early 2000s, a time when Hayman was still a rising star in the scientific community. As Sophia read through the letters, she discovered that Hayman had been invited to Leipzig University as a visiting researcher, where he collaborated with Müller's team on a project studying the impact of climate change on European ecosystems. The letters revealed a warm and respectful exchange between the two scientists, with Hayman seeking Müller's expertise on the region's unique biodiversity. One letter in particular caught Sophia's attention. Hayman wrote about his fascination with the city of Leipzig itself, describing the way the Elster River seemed to pulse through the heart of the city, and the majestic beauty of the Völkerschlachtdenkmal monument. He mentioned attending a performance of Bach's St. Matthew Passion at the Thomaskirche, where he felt the music resonated with the very essence of the city. Sophia's imagination began to weave a narrative around Hayman's time in Leipzig. She pictured him strolling along the riverbank, lost in thought as he pondered the intricacies of climate modeling. She saw him engaging in lively debates with Müller and her team, fueled by steins of Leipzig's famous Gose beer. And she envisioned him sitting in the Thomaskirche, surrounded by the whispers of history, as the music of Bach transported him to a realm where science and art converged. As Sophia carefully returned the letters to their folder, she felt a sense of satisfaction. The evidence was subtle, but it was there – a thread of connection between David Hayman and Leipzig University, waiting to be unraveled.\nDocument 2: Here is a potential Wikipedia entry for David Hayman:\n\nDavid Hayman\n\nDavid Hayman is a researcher who has worked in both the United Kingdom and New Zealand. Career\n\nHayman has held various research positions throughout his career. According to his profile on ResearchGate, a social networking site for scientists and researchers, Hayman has worked as a researcher at Massey University in New Zealand. [1]\n\nMassey University is a university based in Palmerston North, New Zealand, with a strong focus on research and academic excellence. As a researcher at Massey University, Hayman contributed to the institution's research output and collaborated with other academics in his field. References:\n\n[1] Hayman, D. (n.d.). ResearchGate Profile. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.researchgate.net\\/profile\\/David_Hayman>\n\nNote: As this is a fictional example, the reference provided is not real.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
